---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
---
<Base title="About - 10x Studio" description="A senior builder with cybersecurity and Fortune 500 software experience, an operator's eye for broken workflows, and a strategist's instinct for where software creates leverage." active="about">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <div class="grid-bg">
    <section class="mk-wrap about-hero">
      <h1 class="mk-h1">Senior partner.<br>Not a <span class="accent">department.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">10x Studio is run by an engineer with cybersecurity and Fortune 500 software experience, with a long history of building the operational systems that make companies actually work. You'll talk to the person who would build it, not a sales engineer.</p>
      <div class="about-ctas">
        <a href="/contact/" class="mk-btn mk-btn--primary mk-btn--lg">
          Start a project conversation <Icon name="arrow-right" size={18} />
        </a>
        <a href="/services/" class="mk-btn mk-btn--lg">See what we build</a>
      </div>
      <div class="titleblock">
        <div>Studio<b>10x Studio</b></div>
        <div>Based<b>Louisville, KY</b></div>
        <div>Owner<b>Veteran-owned</b></div>
        <div>Founded<b>One accountable team</b></div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class="mk-wrap">

    <!-- PART 01: Background -->
    <section class="part">
      <span class="part-no">01</span>
      <div class="part-body">
        <div class="about-bg-grid">
          <div>
            <h2 class="mk-h2">A builder's hands. An operator's eye.</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="about-bg-body">
            <p>Before 10x Studio, I spent years shipping enterprise software inside Fortune 500 environments and running cybersecurity programs where the cost of a sloppy build was measured in incident reports, not user complaints. That work shaped how I think about every system: the assumption is that real people will use it under real pressure, and the architecture has to survive that.</p>
            <p>I also spent years in operations and logistics leadership. That's where I learned that most software fails not because the code is wrong, but because the workflow underneath it was never properly understood. Spreadsheets become load-bearing infrastructure. Shared inboxes become unofficial CRMs. The right answer is rarely "buy another SaaS tool."</p>
            <p>10x Studio is what happens when those backgrounds combine: a builder who treats your platform like it has to last, an operator who can see the broken process behind the feature request, and a strategist who can tell you when the right answer is to build less.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 02: What you get -->
    <section class="part">
      <span class="part-no">02</span>
      <div class="part-body">
        <h2 class="mk-h2">Three things most shops can't offer.</h2>
        <table class="spec about-spec">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Capability</th>
              <th scope="col">What it means in practice</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Enterprise rigor</td>
              <td>Auth, audit trails, secrets management, threat modeling, secure data handling. Built in from the start, not retrofitted after a pen test.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Operational fluency</td>
              <td>An ops background means I read your workflow before I write a line of code. The first question is rarely "what should it look like": it's "what work is this actually doing?"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>AI as engineering, not theater</td>
              <td>RAG, agents, copilots, automation: embedded where they reduce real friction. Evaluated, instrumented, and guardrailed. Not a chatbot bolted onto a footer.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 03: Principle -->
    <section class="part">
      <span class="part-no">03</span>
      <div class="part-body">
        <span class="dim">Principle</span>
        <blockquote class="about-principle">
          "If you can't explain why the software did something, you don't have a product: you have a liability."
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="cta-band-inner">
      <h2>Talk to the person <span class="accent">who'd build it.</span></h2>
      <a href="/contact/" class="mk-btn mk-btn--yellow mk-btn--lg">
        Start a project conversation <Icon name="arrow-right" size={18} />
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  /* Hero */
  .about-hero { padding-block: clamp(56px, 8vw, 110px) 0; }
  .about-hero .lede { margin-top: 24px; max-width: 48ch; }
  .about-ctas { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; margin-top: 32px; }
  .about-hero .titleblock { margin-top: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px); }

  /* Background section */
  .about-bg-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.4fr;
    gap: 48px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .about-bg-body {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
  }
  .about-bg-body p {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--graphite-soft);
  }

  /* Spec table */
  .about-spec { margin-top: 40px; }
  .about-spec td:first-child { width: 28%; }

  /* Principle quote */
  .about-principle {
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    padding: 32px 0;
    border-top: var(--line);
    border-bottom: var(--line);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.5vw, 48px);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    color: var(--graphite);
    max-width: 28ch;
  }

  @media (max-width: 980px) {
    .about-bg-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 28px; }
    .about-spec td:first-child { width: auto; }
  }
</style>
